ctually think  they're doing that badly on that front  but I mean again there's twice as many  drugs out there that sort of haven't  gone through this evidentiary process so  those are all coming you know those will  all come with time  um research tends to gallop faster than  um than safety and that and then sort of  FDA and that that sort of makes sense  you know the FDA associated with safety  and research is about finding stuff out  so I think in time uh one will catch up  with the other and we'll have a lot more  information  to give to people  okay great thank you your next question  what are the most important  considerations when choosing an NGS  platform for pharmacogenomics testing  uh that's a great question too I mean  there are you know there's so there's a  couple things going on here there's  clinical considerations like assay  flexibility and accuracy and  reproducibility and then there's also  the business considerations which also  access assay flexibility because  again as research uh identifies new  markers you want to be able to add them  very quickly to to your testing panel  right as new genes get identified or new  drugs get get pulled in you you need  these sort of you need the flexibility  to do that but yeah again you also want  your setup and your per sample run costs  uh to be relatively low and you're going  to need a high level of support because  these are you know these are pretty  complex to set up  turnaround time is important throughput  is important important Financial  flexibility in terms of leasing versus  buying and all those things need to be  balanced when platforms are considered  but those those are the big ones for me  all right your next question  where do you see the value of NGS as a  modality in this space above other  Technologies like PCR  oh okay  um so this is kind of a no-brainer  um some of these some of these genes  have you know specific hot pots Hot Spot  Snips and you can do PCR to individually  identify them but in many of the genes I  showed you know the bcag R1 the the  variance the genetic variants that  people have are spread across the entire  Gene right so in those cases NGS is  going to be way better in terms of  providing comprehensive coverage of risk  identification  and because these risk alleles are  dispersed through the entire Gene at a  low frequency you definitely need an NGS  platform to identify them right the  alternative is you're you know  amplifying hundreds and hundreds of  snips and that's going to change over uh  all the time and very rarely will one of  them show up as opposed to sequencing  the entire Gene and identifying the  variants that are present  all right thank you Jonathan we have  time for a couple more questions  um your next question for labs looking  to enter the pharmacogenomic space what  advice would you give them to help them  succeed  oh what a great question okay so when  when my current lab was getting started  when Jesus was just getting started we  definitely benefited from working with  outside experts to validate our systems  our assays and our reporting so  um Next Generation sequencing is a very  B2B collaborative Endeavor like you are  definitely going to want to uh have a  dedicated experts who really know how to  do it and also do not skimp on it  support uh a single hole genome can  require four terabytes bytes of  information storage and uh PGX runs will  fill up your platform storage quicker  than you can imagine so you need to plan  it accordingly in terms of on-site or  Cloud flexible storage right  again the actual running gets very  routine but the setup can be quite  challenging and you're just you're just  far better off of bringing in experts to  help that out  all right thank you and your last  question around pharmacogenomics what  lessons have you learned over the years  that you wish you knew sooner okay so so  I'll I'll do two so I used to joke that  um uh that they called it next  Generation sequencing to make folks of  us who used to do um you know silver  staining silver sting gels and Counting  nucleotides feel old but it's actually  become hugely simplified right again the  startup is really complicated but once  things are running the process truly  looks plug and play uh compared to  um compared to back in the day I I  compare it to using a toaster versus  building a toaster from scratch  so so you know so don't be daunted by  that just bring in expert  um that's just the way to go and also I  should say these systems they generate  mountains of genetic information  um but clinicians only need to know a  small fraction of it which is the parts  that are clinically actionable so  wherever possible bring in people who  know how to curate that data  um so you're not you know you're not  drowning clients in information that  just isn't relevant  um you know telling someone that they  have a genetic variant of Uncertain  significance does them absolutely no  favors right  um whereas telling them you know here  are the pathological variants and here's  uh what is typically done with them you  know providing that information that is  something people can use and it's all  about providing value to people  thank you Jonathan do you have any final  comments for our audience before we  close today  sure I say this all the time but we are  in a golden age uh of biomedical  Sciences we are going to again as the as  the costs of whole genome sequencing and  whole exome sequencing uh get lower and  lower we are going to generate so much  clinically actionable information that  helps people uh make better decisions  about uh their lives and improves the  opportunity for them to live longer and  healthier lives so it's a wonderful  thing to be part of and I just want to  thank everybody for your time today  well thank you again Jonathan for your  time today and your wonderful  presentation we would also like to thank  lab roots and our sponsor Thermo Fisher  Scientific for underwriting today's  educational webcast before we go I'd  like to thank the audience for joining  us today and for their interesting  questions  we did not have time for today and those  submitted during the on-demand period  will be addressed by our speaker via the  contact information you provided at the  time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand and  labords will alert you via email when  it's available for replay we encourage  you to share that email with your  colleagues who may have missed today's  live event until next time goodbye

